+**IP-124.** [Stated — ⛔ **WARRANT: `File 85`, two-label class; sentence-checked**; ⏳ **OPEN EAR FLAG**] Anchor: **§9 Eucharist — Presence** ⭐⭐⭐ ; cross-ref `IP-59`, `LS-130`, `DQ-1` — ⭐⭐ **THE BLACK RUBRIC AND LOCAL PRESENCE, WITH AQUINAS AS THE AUTHORITY AND AN ECUMENICAL ALIGNMENT NAMED.** On the rubric itself: it was *"illegally inserted"* into the 1552, called Black *"due to the fact that the one who would inserted it meant for it to be a rubric, but it was uh inked in black rather than red"* **[`s256`, speaker `A`, `[19:08]`, `@573,294–573,503`]**; it was not carried into the 1559 or later editions; but *"a modified, more accurate, theologically accurate rubric was inserted in the 1662."* **[`s262`, speaker `A`, `[19:56]`, `@598,214–598,315`]**
+
+On what it denies: *"they're talking about the local idea of presence, a localizing of Christ's body and blood"* **[`s323`, speaker `A`, `[27:06]`, `@798,543–798,642`]**. ⭐⭐ *"On one hand, the Reformed and the Romans actually agree on this, and on the other hand, the Lutherans have a different position on this."* **[`s325`, speaker `A`, `[27:15]`, `@803,053–803,189`]** He grounds it in Aquinas by name — *"Saint Thomas Aquinas himself is making this argument"* **[`s329`, speaker `A`, `[27:53]`, `@816,430–816,509`]** — with the worked illustration *"when you are moving the chalice left and right, you are not moving Christ's body and blood left and right."* And on reception: *"You are receiving the whole of Christ in receiving the supper."* **[`s336`, speaker `A`, `[28:49]`, `@844,612–844,674`]** — *"no one, when receiving the supper, is receiving just a part of Christ."*
+
+**[Analysis]** ⭐⭐ **The `Reformed + Roman against Lutheran` alignment on local presence is a christological, not a eucharistic, division and he says so (*"This is a debate that goes into Christology"*, *"how the divine nature and the human nature interact"*). ⭐ Recorded because it is a rare instance of him placing Anglican eucharistic teaching by naming who ELSE holds it, and doing so across the Reformation divide rather than within one side of it.**
